1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,273
Hello and welcome back to JD Science This

2
00:00:04,288 --> 00:00:08,404
is part one of our Level 3 lesson on transmission at a chemical

3
00:00:08,419 --> 00:00:12,123
synapse Along the axon the signal

4
00:00:12,138 --> 00:00:16,041
is electrical An action potential is a wave

5
00:00:16,056 --> 00:00:19,867
of membrane depolarisation Neurones

6
00:00:19,882 --> 00:00:23,571
do not usually touch the next cell There is

7
00:00:23,587 --> 00:00:27,535
a tiny gap so the message must be converted into a chemical

8
00:00:27,550 --> 00:00:31,590
form to cross it A synapse is the specialised

9
00:00:31,605 --> 00:00:35,279
junction between a neurone and the next cell which may be another

10
00:00:35,294 --> 00:00:39,547
neurone or an effector such as a muscle fibre Look

11
00:00:39,562 --> 00:00:43,221
first at a real electron micrograph of a neuromuscular

12
00:00:43,236 --> 00:00:46,895
junction You can see a packed terminal and a

13
00:00:46,910 --> 00:00:50,766
folded postsynaptic membrane but the individual vesicles

14
00:00:50,782 --> 00:00:54,501
and ions are clearer on a diagram so we now use

15
00:00:54,516 --> 00:00:58,465
a labelled scientific illustration The neurone

16
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:02,474
that arrives is the presynaptic neurone Its

17
00:01:02,489 --> 00:01:06,407
swollen ending is the synaptic knob or synaptic bouton

18
00:01:07,459 --> 00:01:11,651
Inside the knob are mitochondria and many synaptic vesicles

19
00:01:12,702 --> 00:01:16,727
Each vesicle is a membrane sac filled with neurotransmitter

20
00:01:17,779 --> 00:01:21,513
A neurotransmitter is a chemical that carries the signal across

21
00:01:21,529 --> 00:01:25,294
the gap In this lesson the named example

22
00:01:25,309 --> 00:01:29,013
is acetylcholine The gap itself is the

23
00:01:29,029 --> 00:01:33,724
synaptic cleft about twenty nanometres wide The

24
00:01:33,739 --> 00:01:38,968
membrane of the next cell is the postsynaptic membrane Embedded

25
00:01:38,983 --> 00:01:42,977
in it are specific receptor proteins Those

26
00:01:42,992 --> 00:01:46,651
receptors recognise acetylcholine and are linked to ion

27
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:50,309
channels An action potential arrives at

28
00:01:50,324 --> 00:01:53,937
the presynaptic terminal by electrical propagation along the

29
00:01:53,952 --> 00:01:57,641
axon That depolarisation opens

30
00:01:57,657 --> 00:02:02,657
voltage-gated calcium channels in the knob membrane Calcium

31
00:02:02,672 --> 00:02:06,483
ions are far more concentrated outside so they enter

32
00:02:06,498 --> 00:02:10,492
the knob down their electrochemical gradient The

33
00:02:10,507 --> 00:02:14,623
rise in calcium inside the knob is the trigger for vesicle movement

34
00:02:15,675 --> 00:02:19,364
Vesicles are drawn to the presynaptic membrane and fuse

35
00:02:19,379 --> 00:02:23,221
with it Fusion releases acetylcholine into

36
00:02:23,236 --> 00:02:27,733
the cleft by exocytosis Exocytosis

37
00:02:27,748 --> 00:02:31,895
means the vesicle membrane joins the plasma membrane and the contents

38
00:02:31,910 --> 00:02:35,797
are emptied outside the cell Acetylcholine

39
00:02:35,812 --> 00:02:40,721
molecules then diffuse across the synaptic cleft Diffusion

40
00:02:40,736 --> 00:02:45,279
is passive movement from high to low concentration They

41
00:02:45,294 --> 00:02:48,983
bind to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane

42
00:02:50,035 --> 00:02:55,004
Binding opens ligand-gated ion channels Ligand-gated

43
00:02:55,019 --> 00:02:58,709
means the channel is opened by a chemical messenger not by

44
00:02:58,724 --> 00:03:02,459
voltage At a nicotinic acetylcholine

45
00:03:02,474 --> 00:03:06,117
synapse those channels allow sodium ions to enter

46
00:03:06,132 --> 00:03:09,791
the postsynaptic cell Sodium entry

47
00:03:09,806 --> 00:03:13,510
produces a postsynaptic potential a local change in

48
00:03:13,526 --> 00:03:17,138
membrane voltage If enough receptors

49
00:03:17,154 --> 00:03:21,071
are activated and if the depolarisation at the axon hillock

50
00:03:21,087 --> 00:03:25,065
of the next neurone reaches threshold voltage-gated sodium

51
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:29,913
channels open and a new action potential is generated The

52
00:03:29,928 --> 00:03:33,769
electrical signal has been rebuilt on the far side of the gap

53
00:03:34,972 --> 00:03:38,885
Notice the contrast Along the axon

54
00:03:39,145 --> 00:03:43,032
charged ions move through voltage-gated channels and the impulse

55
00:03:43,047 --> 00:03:47,056
is electrical Across the cleft the message

56
00:03:47,071 --> 00:03:51,004
is a chemical acetylcholine travelling by diffusion

57
00:03:52,056 --> 00:03:55,882
That conversion is why a typical chemical synapse is slower

58
00:03:55,898 --> 00:03:59,922
than axonal conduction and why it can be modified by drugs

59
00:03:59,937 --> 00:04:04,495
or enzymes Here is a worked examination-style

60
00:04:04,510 --> 00:04:08,794
example Place these events in order starting

61
00:04:08,809 --> 00:04:13,245
from the moment the action potential arrives Calcium

62
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:18,504
channels open Calcium enters Vesicles

63
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:22,315
fuse Acetylcholine is released by

64
00:04:22,331 --> 00:04:25,974
exocytosis Acetylcholine

65
00:04:25,989 --> 00:04:30,212
diffuses and binds Ligand-gated sodium

66
00:04:30,227 --> 00:04:34,145
channels open A postsynaptic potential

67
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:38,520
forms The order is exactly that sequence

68
00:04:39,571 --> 00:04:43,337
Arrival calcium entry exocytosis

69
00:04:43,581 --> 00:04:47,209
diffusion receptor binding ligand-gated sodium

70
00:04:47,224 --> 00:04:50,959
influx then a postsynaptic potential that may start

71
00:04:50,974 --> 00:04:54,998
a new action potential Now pause the video

72
00:04:55,013 --> 00:04:58,855
and try this On the synapse diagram

73
00:04:59,007 --> 00:05:02,940
decide which structure releases acetylcholine and which structure

74
00:05:02,956 --> 00:05:06,568
binds it Then state whether that arrangement

75
00:05:06,584 --> 00:05:10,273
allows the signal to travel backwards Here

76
00:05:10,288 --> 00:05:13,916
is a model answer Acetylcholine is

77
00:05:13,931 --> 00:05:18,230
released from vesicles in the presynaptic knob It

78
00:05:18,245 --> 00:05:22,834
binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane Because

79
00:05:22,849 --> 00:05:26,812
the vesicles are only on one side and the receptors are only

80
00:05:26,828 --> 00:05:30,715
on the other a typical chemical synapse cannot send the signal

81
00:05:30,730 --> 00:05:34,358
backwards In part two we will see how

82
00:05:34,373 --> 00:05:38,443
acetylcholine is removed how choline is recycled

83
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:43,306
and why that clean-up is essential for repeated one-way transmission

84
